cpp-pinyin2 provides CMake targets:

  find_package(cpp-pinyin2 CONFIG REQUIRED)
  target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE cpp-pinyin2::cpp-pinyin2)

The dictionary tables are installed next to the package config, at `share/cpp-pinyin2/dict`, and the library
expects a dictionary root at run time. It never guesses one: `cppp2_open` / `Engine::open` take the directory
as an argument. Three ways a host provides it, in the order this port recommends:

1. ASK. `cppp2_dict_dir_candidate()` (C ABI, static string, no ownership) reports where THIS build installed
   the tables, resolved against the library's own module directory -- so an application that is relocated keeps
   working. A CMake consumer already has the same answer as the variable `CPP_PINYIN2_DICT_DIR`. Pass that path on.

2. COPY the tables next to your executable at build time, if the application ships its own directory tree
   (an installer needs the files anyway):

     add_custom_command(TARGET main POST_BUILD
         COMMAND "${CMAKE_COMMAND}" -E copy_directory
           "${cpp-pinyin2_DIR}/dict"
           "$<TARGET_FILE_DIR:main>/dict")

3. EMBED them, for a single-file deployment (one .exe, an AppImage, a plugin DLL with no sidecar data):
   build the library with `-DCPP_PINYIN2_EMBED_DICTS=ON` (or `=mandarin` for one dialect). The tables become
   part of the library, and a request for a path that does not exist is answered from that copy. This port does
   not build that way by default -- it is a consumer decision, not a packaging one.

A directory that a path points at ALWAYS wins over an embedded copy, so an application can still override at
run time (unset the path and the embedded tables answer).

The same tables are also available through pkg-config (cpp-pinyin2.pc, `dictdir=`) for non-CMake builds.

Note: this port is `cpp-pinyin2`. The 1.x line is a separate port (`cpp-pinyin`, frozen at 1.0.2) with a
different package name, target name and header layout; the two can be installed at the same time.
